WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.150
Those swords are made really well,

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.370
because they're layered
bits of bamboo together.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.940
I think it would have to
be a really perfect storm

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.410
for that to actually happen,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.590
seeing as how these aren't very sharp.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:19.928
Hi, my name is Thekla Hutyrova.

00:00:19.928 --> 00:00:22.590
I am a stuntwoman and
martial arts world champion,

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.320
and I've been doing
nunchucks and martial arts

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.950
in general for about 18 years.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.270
Today, we'll be looking at
nunchuck scenes in movies

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.712
and judging how real they are.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.170
It's very traditional nunchuck style.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.400
Has a lot of the
passing-under-the-hand stuff,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.750
which is super cool and more old school.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.080
The longer chain ones
might be more practical.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.820
The longer the chain, the
further your reach is.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.023
This is about the length, I think,

00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:50.600
that they were in that last clip.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.080
'Cause the ones I compete
with, it's a shorter chain,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.139
so you can do more
flourishy things with it.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.030
Not only did he hold it normal grip,

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.490
which is your thumb facing
the chain, pretty much,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.270
but whenever he switched his hand,

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:08.160
he had them backwards a few times.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.300
You don't see that often.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.420
It's a style. I think
it's like knife, you know,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.190
you have your regular standard grip,

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.320
and then you have ice pick,
where it's upside down.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.560
So I guess that'd be like
the ice pick version,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.393
which is cool.

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:19.660
This is the normal grip,

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.050
but it's neat to see it this way,

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.633
'cause obviously it works the same way,

00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:24.366
but it's just cool.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.470
You don't have to start under the armpit.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.060
I think that's another
just traditional move.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.354
They usually spin it,
catch it under the armpit,

00:01:35.354 --> 00:01:37.010
and then shoot it back out again.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.820
And I think maybe that's
more of a sneaky way to do it

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.640
as opposed to being loaded here, right?

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.817
You're here, and then everyone's like,

00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:44.273
"Oh, there's no way," and then shoop!

00:01:44.273 --> 00:01:47.010
Traditional weapons especially
come from farm tools.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.025
This was just for smacking grain

00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:51.270
out of hay and wheat and stuff.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.347
And then someone's like,
"Hmm, this works on grain.

00:01:53.347 --> 00:01:55.630
I wonder what it'll do to someone's face."

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.062
I can't rate Bruce Lee! 10!

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.061
All right, maybe 9, 'cause he
showed off for way too long.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.770
You can kind of see a
little bit in some of it

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.720
where maybe the actress isn't completely

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.680
that well versed with the nunchucks.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.710
They're a little bit slower.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.220
She's kind of using more of her body

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.800
and more of her arms and stuff

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.370
instead of just moving the
nunchuck very skillfully,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.030
but obviously she's quite skilled.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.320
There's a few cool moves, though.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.860
The neck, sliding under
the legs and hitting him.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:32.693
It's pretty cool.

00:02:32.693 --> 00:02:35.242
But it's definitely not as realistic

00:02:35.242 --> 00:02:39.210
to think that she wouldn't
just be grabbed or something.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.400
She's being a lot more calculating

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.859
and choreographed with it.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.822
The last move, where she puts
it on her foot and kicks it,

00:02:48.822 --> 00:02:52.000
I've seen actually done in form.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.240
I'd say, like, 7. But I still love it.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.690
Oh, double!

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.010
There we go.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.830
He's just doing the same
combo over and over.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.663
I love it.

00:03:04.663 --> 00:03:06.660
It's funny how, often,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.760
it's just, like, the
demonstration of the weapon,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:10.777
especially with nunchucks,

00:03:10.777 --> 00:03:14.270
is the bit that is so highlighted.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:15.410
He did a good job.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.830
Like, obviously it was him doing it,

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.447
and the move where he crosses
them isn't very easy to do.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.450
If I were to be in a fight with nunchucks,

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.020
I think I would just try to
make the big swinging strikes.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.110
The best one's probably from
top to bottom at the angles.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.040
You could do a side to
side or a nice uppercut.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.280
Anything else ends up
being a lot more specific.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.220
Also, I mean, groin, knees,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.470
those are always defaults
for self-defense moves,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:49.060
and a nunchuck to the kneecap
wouldn't be very comfortable.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.730
So that'd probably be a good one too.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.040
So what he did, you know,
just wailing on him,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.040
was probably a really good default.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:01.140
I want to say they're foam
ones in that scene too,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.570
just by the weight that
it looked like they had,

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:04.580
when it bounced back.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.210
"Real" nunchucks are made out of wood.

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.920
They're illegal in California.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.840
It's just always such
an interesting question

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.624
that people ask that kind
of confuses me sometimes,

00:04:14.624 --> 00:04:16.323
about, "Are they real?"

00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:17.189
If you're training with a sword,

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:18.710
if it's made out of metal, it is.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:21.530
But if it's not sharp,
then I guess it's not real.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.410
Like, these are real, they're just foam.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.360
So it's like a weird, you know,

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.250
like, what the definition
of a "real" weapon is.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.640
He just did the demonstration,
which was really cool.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.090
But the actual fight bit
was, like, three moves,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:33.800
and the nunchuck bounced back.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.153
So, like, a 5.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.980
Yes, concealed-carry nunchucks!

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:44.813
So cool.

00:04:44.813 --> 00:04:48.340
I've been known to just keep
them in a backpack or a purse

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.090
'cause I forgot that they're there,

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.527
so they are very concealable.

00:04:54.388 --> 00:04:55.554
Whew!

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.400
That's dope.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.660
I love that he used it

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.730
to take on a bunch of
people surrounding him,

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.370
'cause I feel like that's
probably the best use of it,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.687
just 'cause if you spin
around fast enough,

00:05:10.687 --> 00:05:11.940
people aren't going to want to attack you.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.692
I think that is a really
good use of a flaily weapon.

00:05:15.692 --> 00:05:17.970
If he had a stick, someone might grab it,

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.473
disarm him, or, you know.

00:05:19.473 --> 00:05:24.470
It's an easier thing to
catch when it's one stick.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.880
But when it's a nunchuck, it's terrifying.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.600
It's scary for the person wielding it,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.275
much less the one in front.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.350
Those swords are made really well,

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:41.570
because they're layered
bits of bamboo together.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.140
I think it would have to
be a really perfect storm

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.600
for that to actually happen,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.870
seeing as how these aren't very sharp.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:53.600
But it's Donnie Yen, so,
you know, it's possible.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.330
If they wanted to swarm him,

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.170
they could've beaten him.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.877
But I think it's a really
good way to capture

00:06:01.877 --> 00:06:04.700
the efficiency and the
use of the actual weapon.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.770
So, I don't know, 9.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.935
A fellow chucker, eh?

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.240
Yeah, I mean, rolling it over
your hand is always harder,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.090
'cause it involves
letting go of one of them

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.220
and just letting it roll over your hand

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.200
and get to the other one.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.830
It's hard to do with the longer chain,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.870
because you have to be able to get that.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.660
So I'd say straight
strikes are the easiest,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:28.870
and then something with a spin,

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:30.090
and then something with a release

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.470
as far as difficulty tiers.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:36.400
So that difficulty scale
and the speed, I think,

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.828
are pretty good signs of expertise.

00:06:40.727 --> 00:06:42.840
You gotta love "Ninja Turtles."

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.840
It's like the dueling pianos thing.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.318
Keep practicing!

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.780
See, this is where
nunchucks get a bad rap.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.927
They're like, "Oh, no one's actually doing

00:06:53.927 --> 00:06:56.180
anything with them," and
then the guy with the stick

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.850
comes in and beats everyone up.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.980
But if they actually got close enough

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.270
where chucks would hit someone,

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.957
maybe the guy with the bo staff
wouldn't have to be there.

00:07:05.957 --> 00:07:08.850
They showed some good
expertise with the nunchucks,

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.270
but there was zero fight,
actual interaction,

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.970
so I'll have to give it a 2 or a 3.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:18.029
But again, for the demonstration
bit, maybe a 6 or 7.

00:07:23.897 --> 00:07:26.620
All right. Ooh, pass
around the back, nice.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.240
Again, with this, you see a
lot of just straight strikes,

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:30.473
not so much fancy stuff

00:07:30.473 --> 00:07:31.964
when you're actually
interacting with people,

00:07:31.964 --> 00:07:34.420
which, again, it's the diagonal strikes,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.020
the sideways ones, upwards.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.410
Hitting the guy out of the air

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.720
was probably the least practical thing.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.610
I still liked Donnie Yen's version better,

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.760
with everyone attacking
at once, 'cause, again,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.160
this was a little more choreographed,

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.780
where, like, all the bad guys come in

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.605
at their designated time,

00:07:49.605 --> 00:07:51.296
which is plausible, of course,

00:07:51.296 --> 00:07:52.540
but I think if you're a mob

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:54.100
trying to end up hurting someone,

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.741
you just go like, "One,
two, three, let's go!"

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.370
Finishes them off of a kick.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:08.069
He used a lot more martial arts kicks

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:09.769
and other movement

00:08:09.769 --> 00:08:11.743
as opposed to just swinging
the nunchuck around,

00:08:11.743 --> 00:08:14.260
because obviously someone
who can do nunchucks

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.880
should have some
background in martial arts.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.330
It made it feel more realistic
because of that as well.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.310
The strikes were awesome.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:24.990
But then there were a few of
the more intricate things,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.440
like when he wrapped the guy's arm,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.800
which I feel like took a little longer

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.060
than it probably practically would.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:34.530
Like, if someone's punching,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.370
you don't really have all
that time for that to happen.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.040
It's plausible, of course.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.490
While it still felt very
specifically choreographed,

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.230
it was lot more believable
than the "Kick-Ass" one,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.696
where there were just
so many specialty moves.

00:08:53.463 --> 00:08:57.970
Would really suck to get cracked
across the jaw or the eye.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.570
Obviously, a sharp weapon is
more practical in a real fight

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.430
if you're actually wanting
to hurt people that bad.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.380
But I do think, if you
know what you're doing,

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.530
they end up being like a stick.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:11.730
I used to think that they're
not as effective or as good

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:13.350
because I thought they'd bounce back.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.880
But, again, if you don't hit
with anything but the last bit,

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.510
it just whips by a little bit,

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.940
and then you have a lot
of power in that last bit.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.340
Maybe not the easiest thing
to use in a real fight,

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.270
but I do think they could
probably be pretty devastating

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:26.530
if you know how to use them right.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:28.813
Maybe, like, a 7.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:32.120
That would suck!

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.020
I feel like the physics of
that doesn't quite work,

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:35.361
but it's a cool move, you know?

00:09:40.551 --> 00:09:42.800
I liked the Bruce Lee character dodging.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.170
He's very skilled to dodge
that close to the nunchucks,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:48.180
but I liked him going, stepping off line,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.450
and punching the guy and
taking the nunchucks,

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.720
which is what you want to do with

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.353
disarming a weapon of any sort, right?

00:09:53.353 --> 00:09:54.880
You want to kind of get in past it

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.276
and get past that end
where the most power is.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.930
There was one bit where he hits the pipe

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:06.590
and then hits the guy's hand,

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:10.270
which is really a cool take on it,

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:12.740
because hitting fingers would suck.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:14.940
Whereas you're not
going to disarm the pipe

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.940
out of someone's hand.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.070
Important muscles for using nunchucks,

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.680
one of the main ones
is actually your wrist

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.120
for the more intricate movements,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.562
and then obviously your arm and your back,

00:10:23.562 --> 00:10:26.410
and power comes from your hips.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.430
Range of motion is very important,

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.520
arguably more important than muscle mass.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.740
This one's tough, 'cause I liked this one.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.230
It showed a variety of styles,

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.113
but if we have to rate it on practicality,

00:10:38.113 --> 00:10:39.894
7 or something.

00:10:39.894 --> 00:10:41.481
Nunchucks.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:46.070
These people need to start

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.190
holding on to their
weapons a little better.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.540
Yeah, it's funny to keep seeing that theme

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.741
of just people taking
nunchucks from other people

00:10:54.741 --> 00:10:56.120
in all of these.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.500
I've never noticed that, until
you have them back to back.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:10:59.950
You're like, "What is going on?"

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.760
I don't know if I would
block an ax with it,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.790
just because I'm not
sure how sharp the ax is

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.700
and how heavy, and it's
kind of a small chain.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:14.430
So it's not unlikely
that it might snap it.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:16.070
There's also other blocks.

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.460
This ends up actually
being a block as well,

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:20.920
because you're here,
you're blocking all that,

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:22.900
and then you can also kind of block

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.710
if you just hold them,

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.120
their stick on your arm,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.300
so that's a good block as well.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:28.475
Again, I don't think they're

00:11:28.475 --> 00:11:30.983
the most secure things to block with,

00:11:30.983 --> 00:11:32.290
but that's not a horrible move.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.350
Especially if you're
stepping off to the side,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:38.520
just to ensure you're going to not be hit

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:39.750
if something does happen.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.370
Probably parry instead of
just block straight on.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:43.938
I don't think it showed off

00:11:43.938 --> 00:11:47.077
the practicality of
nunchucks all that well.

00:11:47.077 --> 00:11:48.490
You'd have to be very skilled

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:50.000
and very specific with your movement

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.890
to be able to pull that off at all,

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:53.120
and I wouldn't recommend it.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:54.100
So maybe, like, a 5.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.500
But it was a cool little scene.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.097
Ooh, there's that move again.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.830
I mean, he obviously knows what he's doing

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:05.733
based on our criteria from earlier,

00:12:05.733 --> 00:12:07.791
like the intricacy, he's switching hands,

00:12:07.791 --> 00:12:09.674
and the speed at which he's moving them

00:12:09.674 --> 00:12:11.160
and the spins and everything.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.530
So that's really cool to see.

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:16.558
I grip it

00:12:16.558 --> 00:12:20.070
maybe a thumb width away
from the chain and the top,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.930
because that's where you
have the most control

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.960
over the other stick as well.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:28.270
I guess for distance and
just the straight strikes,

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:29.750
you might grip it a little lower.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.010
And then also depending on the
intricacy of your movement,

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:33.230
sometimes for some moves,

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.950
you want to grip it a little bit lower

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:38.020
so that you can get
around your hand for that,

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:39.500
or you want to grip it a little higher

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:41.220
if you're doing a finger
spin or something.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:42.290
But generally speaking,

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:44.200
you want to be close to the chain on top.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:45.220
And then all you have to worry about

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:48.210
is where the chain is
flinging the rest of it.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:49.318
So it's a lot more controlled.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:54.930
I don't do the eyes-fluttering thing.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.970
It is kind of indicative
of not knowing the distance

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.500
and location of the nunchucks,
or any weapon, really.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.630
I think people do that
with bo staff and sword

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.850
and stuff as well, even if it's just foam.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.790
If I throw it and lose track of it,

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.130
then I do that, obviously.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:15.000
But I do know where they're
going most of the time.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.410
But you definitely hit
yourself quite a bit,

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:18.380
especially at first when
you're still learning

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.640
and if you're not being smooth.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:23.480
A lot of times it's funny
bone, which is fantastic;

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:24.580
back of the head,

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.820
which, I'm not even sure
how that gets there,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:27.653
but that's a good one.

00:13:27.653 --> 00:13:29.000
Also makes a really good sound.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.100
They're definitely special,

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:34.620
but I've never knocked
myself out with them

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:36.538
or anything horrible.

00:13:36.538 --> 00:13:39.950
So, practicality-wise, I mean,
probably the 4 or 5 again,

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:42.220
just 'cause some of the strikes

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:44.620
didn't look like they hit exactly.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:51.420
Oh, this is the quintessential scene.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.047
I can't rag on this. Canceled immediately.

00:13:54.047 --> 00:13:57.160
I feel like it is realistic

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.940
how few times they hit
each other with them

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.570
and how much more it is
kind of like a distraction.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:04.850
Like, that first move he did,

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:07.080
he does, Bruce Lee does a distraction move

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:10.520
and then kicks him in the
ankle to throw him off.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:12.670
So that stuff's more practical, I think.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:16.780
It felt maybe not, like, gritty-realistic,

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.963
but it did feel realistic within, like,

00:14:18.963 --> 00:14:20.205
if you're sparring with someone,

00:14:20.205 --> 00:14:22.288
you're going to show off
your moves and everything

00:14:22.288 --> 00:14:26.210
and kind of test the
waters and the distance,

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.990
which they were doing a lot

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.950
during the demonstration periods,

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.152
and just kind of showing off.

00:14:31.152 --> 00:14:34.580
Very simple, straight-across
strikes, which would work.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.210
But yeah, I think that's
one of the best ones

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:37.420
we've seen for sure.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.253
I'd say that's the 10.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.820
So, the spear kind of beat the
nunchucks in that, I think,

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.040
even though she won.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:57.270
Definitely less intricate movement.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.063
It was kind of nice that there wasn't

00:14:59.063 --> 00:15:02.080
a demonstration of skill in this one.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:04.040
It was just like, it
went right to the fight.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.892
Maybe the nunchucks
weren't the right choice

00:15:05.892 --> 00:15:08.470
for that girl to use in
that fight against a spear.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:09.578
It wasn't bad.

00:15:09.578 --> 00:15:13.570
I just think the other ones
obviously were much stronger

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.665
showing the benefit of nunchucks

00:15:16.665 --> 00:15:20.170
and the practicality of
it and the impact of them,

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:22.410
'cause even in this, she hit
the girl across the face,

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:25.760
but there wasn't a lot of impact

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.650
on how that would actually feel.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:30.380
And I think the distance, again,

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:33.710
looked a little off
because they were so close.

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:34.980
So a lot of her strikes ended up

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:36.230
being a little weaker-looking.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:37.960
The distance felt a little off,

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:41.400
and the filming didn't
quite showcase the nunchucks

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.339
as well as some of the
other clips would have,

00:15:43.339 --> 00:15:45.070
so maybe a 4 or a 3.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:45.990
Can you imagine? I'm like,

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:47.518
"I've been in 100 nunchuck fights."

00:15:47.518 --> 00:15:48.370
Like, what?

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:49.640
Thanks so much for watching.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:51.705
If you enjoyed this, click the link above.

